  "name": "CARBOHYDRATE DEFICIENT TRANSFERRIN (CDT)",
  "code": "1054",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"138\" data-end=\"548\"><strong data-start=\"138\" data-end=\"182\">Carbohydrate Deficient Transferrin (CDT)</strong> is a specialized blood test used primarily to detect <strong data-start=\"236\" data-end=\"273\">chronic heavy alcohol consumption</strong>. Transferrin is a glycoprotein that normally has multiple carbohydrate (sialic acid) chains. In individuals who consume large amounts of alcohol regularly, the liver produces transferrin molecules with fewer carbohydrate chains &mdash; termed \"carbohydrate-deficient transferrin.\"</p>\n<p data-start=\"550\" data-end=\"568\"><strong data-start=\"550\" data-end=\"568\">Clinical Uses:</strong></p>\n<ul data-start=\"569\" data-end=\"841\">\n<li data-start=\"569\" data-end=\"643\">\n<p data-start=\"571\" data-end=\"643\">Detect <strong data-start=\"578\" data-end=\"603\">chronic alcohol abuse</strong> (especially &gt;50&ndash;80 g/day for 1&ndash;2 weeks)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"644\" data-end=\"730\">\n<p data-start=\"646\" data-end=\"730\">Monitor <strong data-start=\"654\" data-end=\"679\">abstinence or relapse</strong> in patients under treatment for alcohol dependence</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"731\" data-end=\"786\">\n<p data-start=\"733\" data-end=\"786\">Evaluate <strong data-start=\"742\" data-end=\"763\">liver dysfunction</strong> related to alcohol use</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"787\" data-end=\"841\">\n<p data-start=\"789\" data-end=\"841\">Sometimes used in <strong data-start=\"807\" data-end=\"841\">legal or employment screenings</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong data-start=\"807\" data-end=\"841\">Elevated Levels &ndash; Clinical Implications</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Significance</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Chronic heavy alcohol consumption</strong></td>\n<td>CDT levels rise with regular, heavy alcohol intake&mdash;typically &ge;4&ndash;5 standard drinks/day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Relapse in abstinent individuals</strong></td>\n<td>A rising CDT level may indicate a return to alcohol use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Congenital disorders of glycosylation (rare)</strong></td>\n<td>Can cause elevated CDT, but usually accompanied by neurological or developmental symptoms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>End-stage liver disease</strong> (less common)</td>\n<td>May mildly affect CDT levels due to altered glycosylation patterns</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
